2186442093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442093 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442093 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442093 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442093 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d042f82c-4cb1-56b8-b08d-5af028f1c43a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442094 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ebe11f80-e67f-5114-a62e-afbd040a0f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-169405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1071 "A dough weighs $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ Of this amount, $24.5%$ represents the weight of wheat flour in the dough. How much wheat flour is in the dough?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e9d26ed-5508-5ed1-9b21-0aeebb8bd487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442095 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the dough, which is $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $24.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n24.5%=24.5÷100=0.245\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =0.245\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n24.5%=\\frac{24.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =\\frac{24.5}{100}\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =24.5\\times \\frac{1,400}{100} \\\\  & =24.5\\times 14 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1071 "A party-planning company decorated a room with $200$ balloons. Of this amount, $7.5%$ of the balloons are white. How many white balloons are there?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37153dd3-f5a5-5cb4-89d6-f95a95fd428d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442096 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of balloons in the room, which is $200.$ The percentage is $7.5%,$ and the part is the number of white balloons.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n7.5%=7.5÷100=0.075\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =7.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.075\\times 200 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.5%$ of $200$ is $15.$ Thus, there are $15$ white balloons.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n7.5%=\\frac{7.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =7.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{7.5}{100}\\times 200 \\\\  & =7.5\\times \\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =7.5\\times 2 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.5%$ of $200$ is $15.$ Thus, there are $15$ white balloons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1071 "A restaurant originally had enough capacity to serve $80$ customers. Following a renovation, the restaurant increased its capacity to $120%$ of the original. What was the capacity of the restaurant after the renovation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ab23143-9ffc-5442-9bdb-5fa4de482d26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442097 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the original number of customers the restaurant was able to receive, which is $80$ customers. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of customers the restaurant can receive after the renovation.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 80 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 80 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1071 "In Maria's class, there are $30$ children. Of this amount, $10%$ walk to school. How many children walk to school?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52b2f721-25f8-5813-9d4e-57bac8db769b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442098 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of children in Maria's class, which is $30.$ The percentage is $10%,$ and the part is the number of children who walk to school.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n10%=10÷100=0.1\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =0.1\\times 30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n10%=\\frac{10}{100}=\\frac{10÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1071 "Emma earns $$19,500$ a year. Of this amount, she must pay $12.6%$ in taxes. How much money should Emma pay in taxes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53cc30e1-a9ba-5d93-9a6b-7ee8476d4881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442099 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Emma earns annually, which is $$19,500.$ The percentage is $12.6%,$ and the part is the amount she must pay in taxes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.6%=12.6÷100=0.126\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =0.126\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.6%=\\frac{12.6}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.6}{100}\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =12.6\\times \\frac{19,500}{100} \\\\  & =12.6\\times 195 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1071 "A bunch of grapes contained $50$ grapes. Serena eats $28%$ of them. How many grapes did Serena eat?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a809928-75f9-5540-949c-59cd2e66a5d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442100 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $50$ grapes. The percentage is $28%,$ and the part is the number of grapes Serena ate.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n28%=28÷100=0.28\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =28%\\times 50 \\\\  & =0.28\\times 50 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $28%$ of $50$ is $14.$ Thus, Serena ate $14$ grapes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n28%=\\frac{28}{100}=\\frac{28÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{7}{25}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =28%\\times 50 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25}\\times 50 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{50}{25} \\\\  & =7\\times 2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $28%$ of $50$ is $14.$ Thus, Serena ate $14$ grapes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1071 "In $2018$, Elena earned a salary of $$12,000.$ The following year, she earned $110%$ of the salary received in $2018.$ What was Elena's salary in $2019?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98c55f63-1a03-5c99-847f-6fc5e622bbcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442101 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Elena earned in $2018$, which is $$12,000.$ The percentage is $110%,$ and the part is the amount of money she earned in $2019.$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n110%=110÷100=1.1\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =110%\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =1.1\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =13,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110%$ of $12,000$ is $13,200.$ So, Elena earned $$13,200$ in $2019.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n110%=\\frac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =110%\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =110\\times \\frac{12,000}{100} \\\\  & =110\\times 120 \\\\  & =13,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110%$ of $12,000$ is $13,200.$ So, Elena earned $$13,200$ in $2019.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1071 "$50%$ of $24$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes. How many students have brown eyes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b046c04a-6183-5340-90ee-d461156c145c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442102 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of students in Joan's class, which is $24.$ The percentage is $50%,$ and the part is the number of students that have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n50%=50÷100=0.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.5\\times 24 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n50%=\\frac{50}{100}=\\frac{50÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $35$ children in the fifth-grade class. If $40%$ of them have black hair, how many children have black hair?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b701c4c3-e9fc-563a-b31c-45e1ff282957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442103 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of children in the fifth-grade class, which is $35$ children. The percentage is $40%,$ and the part is the number of children with black hair.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n40%=40÷100=0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 35 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 35 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $40%$ of $35$ is $14.$ So, $14$ children have black hair.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40%=\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 35 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 35 \\\\  & =2\\times \\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =2\\times 7 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $40%$ of $35$ is $14.$ So, $14$ children have black hair." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1071 "A machine produces $200$ light bulbs per day. Of this amount, $1.5%$ of the bulbs are defective. How many bulbs made each day are defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d438f5d5-3cb2-537d-8c22-588564d642dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442104 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of bulbs produced, which is $200.$ The percentage is $1.5%,$ and the part is the number of defective bulbs.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n1.5%=1.5÷100=0.015\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.015\\times 200 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n1.5%=\\frac{1.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{100}\\times 200 \\\\  & =1.5\\times \\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =1.5\\times 2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-169407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1071 "In January $2022,$ a social media account had approximately $53,000$ followers. In December of that year, the account had $120%$ the number of followers it had in January. Determine the number of followers the account had in December, $2022.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7304750-8de2-5c9e-abd7-f379f2372291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442105 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of followers the account had in January, $2022,$ which is $53,000.$ The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of followers the account had in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{53,000}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 10,600 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1071 "A rock band performed at two sold-out concerts. The first concert took place in a location with a $10,000$ people capacity. If the second location's capacity was $115%$ of the first location's capacity, what is the capacity of the second location?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "def47a5b-79f5-55d2-884e-30c4a2ea2af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442106 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first location, which is $10,000$ people. The percentage is $115%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second location.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n115%=115÷100=1.15\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =1.15\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n115%=\\frac{115}{100}=\\frac{115÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{23}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{20}\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =23\\times \\frac{10,000}{20} \\\\  & =23\\times 500 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
